WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.520
A compiler has to keep the math predictable. Add one to

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.877
sixteen million seven hundred seventy-seven thousand

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:09.626
two hundred sixteen in thirty-two-bit floating point,

00:00:09.626 --> 00:00:13.553
and it rounds away. That’s why this ordered sum returns

00:00:13.553 --> 00:00:13.946
four.

00:00:14.646 --> 00:00:17.742
Matrix multiply has a different problem: reusing data.

00:00:17.742 --> 00:00:21.225
Forge works on blocks instead of walking the full

00:00:21.225 --> 00:00:24.708
matrices in one sweep. This example crosses the tile

00:00:24.708 --> 00:00:28.191
boundaries, so the kernel has to finish with smaller

00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:29.739
blocks along the edges.

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:40.857
There’s also a cost to generating machine code in the

00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:43.753
first place. Repeat the same shape and Forge reuses

00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:46.649
that compiled code. Change the length, and the cache

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:49.866
records a miss because the kernel needs to be compiled

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:51.153
for the new shape.
